#: games/categories.rb:1368 games/categories.rb:1382 games/quiz_party.rb:1259 games/quiz_party.rb:1289
msgid "The first round is being prepared."
msgstr ""

msgid "The first seat serves first. Service changes after every two completed points. The table selects a target of 7, 11 or 21 points, but a player also needs a lead of at least two. At 10–10 in an 11-point match, for example, 11–10 is not enough; 12–10 wins. There is a short break after each point before the next serve."
msgstr ""

#: games/axel_pong.rb:259
msgid "The first serve becomes available once the players are connected and ready, without an extra countdown. In a human-only match, you hear the variant, difficulty and target, then who serves. The break after subsequent points remains as described above."
msgstr ""

msgid "The first serve of a human match becomes available after both sides are ready and a three-second countdown. You hear the variant, difficulty and target, then who serves. A bot match starts without this countdown. This does not change the break after subsequent points."
msgstr ""

msgid "The first server is chosen at random once for the match, also against a bot. Service changes after every two completed points. This two-point sequence continues between sets and at deuce; it never changes to one serve each."
msgstr ""

msgid "The first server is chosen at the start of a human match; against a bot, the human starts. Service changes after every two completed points. Choose a target of 7, 11 or 21, with a lead of at least two points required to win. At 10–10 in an 11-point match, 11–10 is not enough; 12–10 wins. After a point there is a three-second break for the goal recording, followed by the score and a further 2.7-second serve delay. You can reposition your paddle during this pause, but serving requires a new press after it ends."
msgstr ""

#: games/audio_ball.rb:208
msgid "The first server is drawn at random once at the start of the match, whether you play a person or a bot. Service then changes after every two completed points. This sequence continues across sets and during play for a two-point lead. A new set does not restart it, and there is no switch to one serve each at deuce."
msgstr ""

msgid "The first starting player is drawn; subsequent starters rotate among remaining players. Each player has a personal train, initially closed, and everyone shares the permanently public Mexican train. A tile must match the end of an accessible train, or the station for an empty train. You may play on your own train, the Mexican train or another open train. Playing on your own closes it; playing on someone else's does not close theirs or yours."
msgstr ""

